<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Allahverdi Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1560–<a href="/years/1613">1613</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Undiladze</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Allahverdi Khan was a Safavid general and statesman of Georgian origin who rose from gholam, military slave, to high office in Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Born a Christian Georgian, he was taken prisoner in a Caucasian campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Shah Tahmasp I</a> and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to serve in the gholam army. After taking part in the killing of the kingmaker Morshed-Kholi Khan in 1589 he became commander of the gholams and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, the first gholam to equal the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, and from 1600 he increased the gholam troops from 4,000 to 25,000. He led campaigns including the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> in 1601–2. The Si-o-se-pol bridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> still bears his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/allahverdi-khan.jpg" alt="Allahverdi Khan" width="330" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1697 artist · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allah_Virdī_Khān,_from_a_Jarūnnāmah_by_Qadrī._Isfahan_-_Safavid_style,_dated_1697.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ghulams replaced most Qizilbash governors, and by the end of his reign a fifth of the high-ranking amirs were ghulams: the Georgian <b>Allahverdi Khan</b> became governor-general of Fars in 1595 and commander-in-chief in 1598.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1601 to 1602 the Safavid general <b>Allahverdi Khan</b> conquered Lar for Shah Abbas I and deposed its last ruler, Ibrahim Khan, and Laristan became a Safavid province.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lar">Kingdom of Lar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Georgia. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1594" aria-label="Isfahan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5876 977 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Allahverdi Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6326 1247h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6326 1247h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1594">What was here in 1594? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
